1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,579
Two agents, one goal, one simulated world.

2
00:00:03,579 --> 00:00:05,425
Then reality changes.

3
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:08,620
At tick six, route R1 closes.

4
00:00:08,620 --> 00:00:12,148
KEEL finds the step that assumed R1 was open,

5
00:00:12,148 --> 00:00:14,707
keeps the three finished steps,

6
00:00:14,707 --> 00:00:16,920
and repairs only the rest,

7
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:20,725
with a recorded model proposal it validates live.

8
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:24,600
The static agent drives on, and learns too late.

9
00:00:26,266 --> 00:00:30,116
Every number here is recomputed from the event log.

10
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:32,887
A failover target degrades.

11
00:00:32,887 --> 00:00:37,525
KEEL re-checks before shifting traffic, and moves checkout to region C.

12
00:00:38,166 --> 00:00:41,986
The static plan sends checkout into the degraded region,

13
00:00:41,986 --> 00:00:43,958
and still reports success.

14
00:00:43,958 --> 00:00:45,991
KEEL never served an error.

15
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:49,687
Now a forged report, and a flapping sensor.

16
00:00:49,687 --> 00:00:51,577
The forgery is rejected.

17
00:00:51,577 --> 00:00:55,925
When the readings oscillate, KEEL pauses, and asks an operator.

18
00:00:56,566 --> 00:00:59,356
One confirmation, and KEEL delivers.

19
00:00:59,356 --> 00:01:03,341
The naive agent followed the noise onto a closed road.

20
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:06,356
Crash the agent mid-step.

21
00:01:06,356 --> 00:01:08,944
It rebuilds from its persisted log:

22
00:01:08,944 --> 00:01:12,100
finished work kept, no effect applied twice.

23
00:01:15,383 --> 00:01:17,529
Tested on SQLite and Postgres,

24
00:01:17,529 --> 00:01:18,781
in the browser,

25
00:01:18,781 --> 00:01:20,808
and under kill-and-takeover.

26
00:01:20,808 --> 00:01:22,358
All of it simulated.

27
00:01:25,833 --> 00:01:26,440
KEEL.

28
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:27,874
The plan can change.

29
00:01:27,874 --> 00:01:29,033
The goal stays.
